\section{Introduction}
Autonomous navigation of marine vessels has witnessed significant progress in recent years, driven by advances in control theory, sensor fusion, and data-driven decision-making~\cite{lv2025unmanned},\cite{qiao2023survey}, ~\cite{alamoush2025maritime}. This is reflected in the growing deployment of unmanned surface vehicles (USVs) for applications ranging from ocean surveying and environmental monitoring to maritime search and rescue and autonomous cargo transport. Despite these developments, achieving robust autonomy in open and confined waterways remains a challenging problem, particularly in the presence of environmental disturbances such as wind, waves, and ocean currents. A foundational requirement for any autonomous marine system is an accurate kinematic model of the vessel, as high-level planning, motion prediction, and feedback control all depend critically on the fidelity of this underlying representation. 

Marine vessels are commonly described by a variety of motion models, including the Unicycle model~\cite{uni}, the Bicycle Kinematic model~\cite{bi}, the Three-DOF Ship model~\cite{3dof}, and the Nomoto model~\cite{fossen}, each capturing different aspects of the vessel's translational and rotational dynamics. The choice of motion model directly influences the accuracy of trajectory prediction and the stability of control actions. In practice, however, the true governing model of a deployed vessel is rarely known a priori, as it depends on hull geometry, propulsion configuration, loading conditions, and hydrodynamic parameters that are difficult to measure directly~\cite{chen2022grey}. This creates a fundamental model uncertainty problem: autonomous systems must operate under assumptions about vessel dynamics that may not hold across different vessels or operating conditions. 

While full dynamic models that account for hydrodynamic forces, added mass, and damping offer greater physical fidelity, their identification requires force and moment measurements that are impractical to obtain during normal vessel operation ~\cite{fossen2011handbook}. Kinematic models, by contrast, describe motion purely in terms of observable pose and velocity quantities, making them directly identifiable from trajectory observations alone without access to force inputs or internal vessel states. This motivates the adoption of kinematic representations as the candidate model set in this work, where classification is performed exclusively from the pose trajectory of the ego ship. 

Existing approaches to motion model identification typically rely on dedicated sensing modalities, white-box system identification procedures, or explicit access to control inputs and onboard inertial measurements~\cite{voss2004nonlinear},~\cite{herrero2012two}. Physics-based identification methods require extensive hydrodynamic testing and model-specific parameter fitting, which limits their generalizability across vessel types~\cite{araki2012estimating}. Sensor-based approaches using IMUs, GPS, or force measurement systems improve estimation accuracy but introduce dependencies on hardware configurations that may not be available in all operational contexts~\cite{alexandersson2022system}. Crucially, the majority of these methods treat motion model identification as a regression or parameter estimation problem~\cite{yoon2003identification}, rather than as a classification task that can be solved directly from observed trajectory data alone~\cite{sebastian2019support}. 



In this work, we propose a data-driven framework for ego ship motion model classification that operates exclusively on the observed pose trajectory of the vessel, without requiring access to explicit control inputs, onboard sensor measurements, or model-specific parameter knowledge. From the raw pose sequence, a set of dynamics-oriented kinematic features is derived, including surge and sway velocities, yaw rate, yaw acceleration, and path curvature, which encode the intrinsic dynamic characteristics of each motion model while remaining invariant to global coordinate frames and operator-specific maneuver styles. These features are processed by a Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) encoder~\cite{bai2018empirical} trained under a joint objective combining supervised contrastive loss~\cite{khosla2020supervised} and cross-entropy loss. The contrastive component enforces intra-class compactness and inter-class separation in the embedding space, while the cross-entropy component directly optimizes class discrimination, enabling the model to generalize across diverse maneuver profiles rather than overfitting to trajectory-specific artifacts. 

The proposed method avoids dependence on image-based or vision-derived inputs, which are susceptible to degradation under adverse maritime conditions such as fog, glare, and low visibility~\cite{bovcon2019mastr1325}, ~\cite{hu2021deep}, ~\cite{prasad2018object}. Instead, classification is performed entirely from the temporal structure of trajectory-derived kinematic features, making the approach applicable across a broad range of operational environments and vessel configurations. A confidence-weighted aggregation scheme further improves trajectory-level prediction robustness by down-weighting ambiguous temporal windows and amplifying high-confidence predictions. The method is validated across four candidate motion models under diverse simulated maneuver conditions. 

